{ id:'opmcq-c14-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'A **58-year-old chronic smoker** presents with drooping of the right eyelid and a **smaller right pupil**, along with **right arm pain and tingling**. Examination shows mild ptosis, miosis and **anhidrosis** on the right side of the face. Chest X-ray reveals a **right apical lung mass**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Third nerve palsy','Horner syndrome due to Pancoast tumor','Myasthenia gravis','Cluster headache'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The complete Horner triad plus an apical lung mass in a smoker is Pancoast tumour until proven otherwise.** This is the reference entry for Horner syndrome in this chapter — `opmcq-c14-9`, `opmcq-c14-10` and `opmcq-c14-24` all lean on it.\n\n**The syndrome** (`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 11; `ophthalmology.pdf p.180`):\n\n| Sign | Mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| **Miosis** | Dilator pupillae denervated — anisocoria **greater in the dark**, with **dilation lag** |\n| **Mild ptosis** | Müller’s muscle (sympathetic accessory elevator) — partial, never complete |\n| **Apparent enophthalmos** | Narrowed palpebral fissure mimics a sunken globe |\n| **Anhidrosis** | Ipsilateral face/neck sudomotor fibres |\n| **Heterochromia** | Congenital cases only |\n\n**Why the lung apex:** the preganglionic (second-order) neurone leaves the cord with the **T1 root and arches over the apex of the lung** on its way up the neck (`L22` slide 10) — so an apical (superior sulcus, Pancoast) tumour invades the sympathetic chain and the lower brachial plexus together. That is why this patient’s Horner comes **with C8–T1 arm pain and tingling**: the combination is the Pancoast signature. `ophthalmology.pdf p.180` lists the aetiology with the life-threatening causes flagged first — **Pancoast’s tumour, thoracic aortic aneurysm, carotid artery dissection, cavernous carotid aneurysm** — and mandates imaging of the whole pathway: "**MRI from the base of the brain to the mid-thoracic region**" (the investigation question at `opmcq-c14-24`).\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Third nerve palsy** | Gives **mydriasis**, complete ptosis and a down-and-out eye (`ophthalmology.pdf p.182`) — every pupil and lid finding here is the reverse. |\n| **Myasthenia gravis** | Fatigable ptosis, yes — but "**the pupil is never affected in MG**" (`ophthalmology.pdf p.185`, printed in capitals), and MG explains neither anhidrosis nor an apical mass. |\n| **Cluster headache** | Can produce a transient Horner during attacks, but there is no headache in this stem and a structural mass on the film trumps a primary headache diagnosis. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Horner syndrome + ipsilateral arm pain + apical lung mass in a smoker = Pancoast tumour invading the T1 sympathetic fibres and lower brachial plexus.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.91 (Ch.14 Q2)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
  stem:'A **67-year-old female** presented with **acute unilateral diminished vision**, **jaw claudication** and severe **temporal headache**, which of the following is the most likely cause of her condition?',
  options:['Non-arteritic optic neuropathy','Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy','Optic neuritis','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Jaw claudication + temporal headache + acute visual loss in a woman over 60 is giant cell arteritis, and its ocular catastrophe is arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION).** The bank keys the generic term — option B — because the *arteritic* qualifier is carried by the vignette itself.\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf p.192` gives arteritic AION = **giant cell (temporal) arteritis**: age **> 60**, **jaw claudication**, **temporal headache and scalp tenderness**, amaurosis fugax, then devastating visual loss with a **chalky white swollen disc**; work-up is **ESR (> 60 mm/hr) and CRP with temporal artery biopsy**, and treatment is **urgent high-dose systemic steroids** to save the fellow eye. The comparison table (`ophthalmology.pdf p.193`):\n\n| | Non-arteritic AION | Arteritic AION (GCA) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Age | **> 50**, atherosclerotic | **> 60** |\n| Systemic symptoms | None | Jaw claudication, headache, scalp tenderness, malaise |\n| Visual loss | Moderate, often on waking, **altitudinal** field defect | Severe — HM/PL |\n| Disc | Hyperaemic swelling, small crowded fellow disc | **Chalky white** swelling |\n| ESR/CRP | Normal | **ESR > 60** |\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 9 shows the disease without naming it — an elderly patient with a **tortuous, beaded temporal artery** and a **superior altitudinal field printout**; the deck prints the words "giant cell arteritis" nowhere, so the systemic vignette here is testable only from the book.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Non-arteritic optic neuropathy** | The trap. NAION is the *commoner* AION, but it is painless and free of systemic symptoms — jaw claudication is a vascular claudication of the masseters and belongs to arteritis alone (`ophthalmology.pdf p.191–193`). |\n| **Optic neuritis** | Disease of **15–45-year-old women**, typically with pain on eye movement, MS-associated in half (`ophthalmology.pdf p.190`) — wrong age, wrong accompaniments. |\n| **None of the above** | B fits exactly. |\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-acute — this is one of the acute painless visual loss presentations.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Acute visual loss + jaw claudication + temporal headache over 60 = arteritic AION from giant cell arteritis; ESR/CRP and urgent high-dose steroids protect the fellow eye.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.91 (Ch.14 Q3)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'A **58-year-old hypertensive man** presents with **sudden diplopia and complete ptosis** of the right eye. The eye is positioned **"down and out,"** and the **pupil is of normal size and reactive**. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Observation only','Start oral steroids','Cerebrovascular assessment (urgent neuroimaging)','Tensilon test'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**A complete third nerve palsy gets vascular imaging even when the pupil is spared.** The pupil rule (`ophthalmology.pdf p.182`) sorts third nerve palsies into two columns — **pupil involved → trauma, aneurysm, tumour** vs **pupil spared → vasculopathy (microvascular infarction in diabetes/hypertension)** — and this hypertensive man with a reactive pupil sits in the benign column. But the rule is a *prior*, not a discharge: `ophthalmology.pdf p.183` prescribes the investigation for third nerve palsy as "**MRI and cerebrovascular imaging to exclude compression from posterior communicating artery aneurysms**" — the one cause that kills, because a PCom aneurysm compressing the nerve is a rupture waiting to happen. The mechanism behind the rule (figure, `ophthalmology.pdf p.182`): the **pupillomotor fibres run superficially** on the nerve, fed by **pial vessels** — a compressing aneurysm crushes them first, while a microvascular infarct of the nerve core tends to spare them. "Tends to" is the operative phrase; sparing is usual, not guaranteed, so a **complete** palsy (total ptosis, down-and-out eye) is imaged regardless.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Observation only** | Reasonable *after* imaging has excluded compression in a vasculopath — never as the first step in a complete palsy. |\n| **Start oral steroids** | No inflammatory diagnosis has been made; steroids treat arteritic AION (`opmcq-c14-3`) and optic neuritis, not a third nerve palsy. |\n| **Tensilon test** | Tests for myasthenia — but MG **never affects the pupil or produces a fixed "down and out" position**, and this palsy pattern is a nerve, not a junction (`ophthalmology.pdf p.185`). |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Complete third nerve palsy warrants urgent MRI + cerebrovascular imaging to exclude a posterior communicating artery aneurysm, even with a spared pupil in a vasculopath.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.91 (Ch.14 Q4)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'A **22-year-old male** presented with double vision, on examination there was **esotropia**, inability to **abduct the left eye** and **face turn to the left side**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Left abducent palsy','Left trochlear palsy','Right abducent palsy','Left abducent palsy','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The lateral rectus is the only abductor, so an eye that cannot abduct has lost its sixth (abducent) nerve.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.184` gives the full picture, and this stem reproduces it line by line: **esotropia** (unopposed medial rectus), **loss of abduction**, horizontal diplopia **worse on gaze toward the palsied side**, and a compensatory **face turn TOWARD the affected side** — turning the face left points both eyes right, taking the paretic left lateral rectus out of work. Left-sided signs + face turn left = **left** abducent palsy. The cause question for this same patient is `opmcq-c14-6`.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Left trochlear palsy** | A vertical problem — hypertropia, weak depression in adduction, diplopia worse on downgaze, head **tilt** rather than face turn (`ophthalmology.pdf p.183`; the very picture printed at `opmcq-c14-7`). |\n| **Right abducent palsy** | Would fail to abduct the **right** eye and turn the face right. |\n| **None of the above** | A fits exactly. |\n\n**Printed defect, recorded not corrected:** the bank prints options A and D as the *same text* — "Left abducent palsy" appears twice — and keys A. Transcribed verbatim; the duplicated option is the bank’s own misprint. *(The same question is reprinted verbatim as ch.15 Q2, duplicate menu and all.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Esotropia + absent abduction + face turn toward the affected side = ipsilateral abducent (VI) nerve palsy.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.92 (Ch.14 Q5; the bank prints options A and D identically)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'In the preceding presentation — a **22-year-old man** with double vision, **esotropia**, inability to **abduct the left eye** and a **face turn to the left** — which of the following is the most probable cause of the patient’s condition?',
  options:['Microvascular infarction','Compressive lesion','Congenital','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Age decides this one.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.184` splits sixth nerve aetiology by decade: **compressive lesions account for 20 % of cases and are especially important in adults UNDER 45** — while **microvascular infarction is the most common cause in patients OVER 55** (diabetes/hypertension, recovering spontaneously over 3–4 months). A 22-year-old with an acquired abducent palsy is squarely in the compressive bracket, which is precisely why the young get imaged and the old vasculopaths may be watched. The book’s other listed causes — **raised intracranial pressure** (a false localizing sign — see `opmcq-c14-19`), **trauma** (the most common cause in children), otitis media (petrositis) and post-viral palsies — round out the differential, but "most probable" in a young adult is the compressive lesion.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Microvascular infarction** | The most common cause **over 55** with vascular risk factors — wrong demographic by three decades. |\n| **Congenital** | The stem describes a new, symptomatic diplopia; a congenital deficit does not present acutely at 22 with double vision (a congenital palsy is suppressed or compensated from childhood). |\n| **All of the above** | Only one of the three fits this patient. |\n| **None of the above** | B fits. |\n\n*(Back-reference repaired: the bank prints "In the previous case…" — the antecedent is Q5 (`opmcq-c14-5`), and the restated stem carries only that case’s findings, not its diagnosis.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'An acquired sixth nerve palsy in a young adult (< 45) is compressive until excluded; microvascular palsy belongs to the over-55 vasculopath.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.92 (Ch.14 Q6, a back-reference to Q5)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'A **34-year-old male** presented with dual vision especially when **looking downwards**, on examination there was **hypertropia**, **weak depression of the left eye when adducted**, **chin depression** and **head tilt to the right side**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Left abducent palsy','Left trochlear palsy','Right abducent palsy','Left abducent palsy','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The superior oblique is the depressor of the ADDUCTED eye — weak depression in adduction is the trochlear (IV) nerve’s signature.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.183` gives every element the stem prints: **hypertropia** of the affected eye (the unopposed inferior oblique lets it ride up), **vertical diplopia worse on downgaze** (reading, descending stairs), and the compensatory posture — **chin depression with face turn and head tilt to the side OPPOSITE the palsy**. Head tilt right = left superior oblique palsy: tilting away from the palsied side lets the intact contralateral muscles level the eyes. Left-sided hypertropia + weak depression of the adducted left eye + right head tilt = **left trochlear palsy**. Its cause question is `opmcq-c14-8`.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Left abducent palsy** (printed twice, as A and D) | A horizontal problem — esotropia, failed abduction, face **turn toward** the palsied side (`opmcq-c14-5`); nothing vertical about it. |\n| **Right abducent palsy** | Same objection, other side. |\n| **None of the above** | B fits exactly. |\n\n**Printed defect, recorded not corrected:** as in Q5, the bank prints "Left abducent palsy" as both option A and option D. Key B as printed. *(This question is reprinted verbatim as ch.15 Q3.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Hypertropia + weak depression of the adducted eye + vertical diplopia worse on downgaze + head tilt to the opposite side = trochlear (IV) nerve palsy.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.92 (Ch.14 Q7; the bank prints options A and D identically)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'In the preceding presentation — a **34-year-old man** with vertical diplopia worse on **looking downwards**, **hypertropia**, weak depression of the **adducted left eye**, chin depression and a **head tilt to the right** — which of the following might be the cause of the patient’s condition?',
  options:['Diabetes','Trauma','Congenital','A & B','All of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Every option on the list is a printed cause of fourth nerve palsy, so "All of the above" carries the key.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.183` lists trochlear aetiology in exactly these terms: **microvascular infarction in diabetes and hypertension**; **trauma** — to which the fourth nerve is uniquely vulnerable (the longest, thinnest cranial nerve, and the only one exiting the brainstem dorsally, so closed head injury classically produces **bilateral** superior oblique palsies); **compressive lesions**; and **congenital** palsy — rare as a listed cause, but a real one, presenting late when the patient’s lifelong compensatory head tilt finally decompensates (old photographs showing the same tilt are the classic clue). Note the phrasing shift from Q6: this stem asks what "**might** be the cause" — possibility, not probability — so it takes the whole list rather than forcing an age-based choice.\n\n| Option | Why it belongs in the key |\n|---|---|\n| **Diabetes** | Microvascular infarction of the nerve — the common vasculopathic cause. |\n| **Trauma** | Classic for CN IV, often bilateral after head injury. |\n| **Congenital** | Printed in the book’s own aetiology list; decompensates in adulthood. |\n| **A & B** | True but incomplete — congenital is a listed cause too. |\n\n*(Back-reference repaired: the bank prints "In the previous case…" — the antecedent is Q7 (`opmcq-c14-7`), and the restated stem carries only that case’s findings, not its diagnosis.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Fourth nerve palsy causes span microvascular (diabetes), trauma (often bilateral), compressive and congenital — "might be the cause" takes them all.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.92 (Ch.14 Q8, a back-reference to Q7)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'A **39-year-old male** presented with **unilateral miosis**, **lid drooping** and **apparent enophthalmos**, which of the following might be the cause for that presentation?',
  options:['Dorsal midbrain syndrome','Argyll-Robertson syndrome','Horner syndrome','Oculomotor nerve palsy','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Unilateral miosis + ptosis + apparent enophthalmos is the Horner triad, read straight off the slide.** `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 11: "Miosis / Dilation Lag / Ptosis / **Apparent enophthalmos** / Anhydrosis / Heterochromia (Congenital)" — "apparent" because the narrowed fissure mimics a sunken globe rather than the eye truly receding. The full syndrome, mechanism and the Pancoast connection are at `opmcq-c14-2`; this same patient’s cause list is `opmcq-c14-10` and his confirmatory investigation is `opmcq-c14-24`.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Dorsal midbrain syndrome** | Upgaze palsy, **light-near dissociation**, convergence-retraction nystagmus and **lid RETRACTION** (Collier sign) — the lid goes the wrong way for this stem (`ophthalmology.pdf p.180`; `opmcq-c14-29`). |\n| **Argyll-Robertson syndrome** | **Bilateral** small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation in neurosyphilis — no ptosis, no enophthalmos, not unilateral (`ophthalmology.pdf p.181`). |\n| **Oculomotor nerve palsy** | Ptosis, yes — but with **mydriasis** and a down-and-out eye, the mirror image of this pupil (`ophthalmology.pdf p.182`). |\n| **None of the above** | C fits exactly. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Unilateral miosis + partial ptosis + apparent enophthalmos = Horner syndrome; ptosis with MYDRIASIS is III palsy, and bilateral irregular light-near-dissociated pupils are Argyll-Robertson.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.92–93 (Ch.14 Q9; stem at the foot of p.92, options on p.93)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'In the preceding presentation — a **39-year-old man** with **unilateral miosis**, **lid drooping** and **apparent enophthalmos** — which of the following might be the cause of the patient’s condition?',
  options:['Pancoast tumor','Thoracic aortic aneurysm','Cervical rib','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All three printed causes sit on the book’s own Horner aetiology list, so the key is "All of the above".** `ophthalmology.pdf p.180` lists the causes with the life-threatening ones flagged first: **Pancoast’s tumour, thoracic aortic aneurysm, carotid artery dissection, cavernous carotid aneurysm** — then neck, thoracic or spinal trauma and surgery, **cervical rib**, demyelinating disease and brainstem infarction. Each option attacks a different station of the pathway drawn on `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 10: the **Pancoast tumour** invades the T1 fibres arching over the lung apex; the **thoracic aortic aneurysm** compresses the sympathetic chain in the chest; the **cervical rib** compresses the stellate/inferior cervical ganglion region at the thoracic outlet. One syndrome, three levels — which is exactly why the confirmatory imaging (`opmcq-c14-24`) has to cover **the base of the brain down to the mid-thoracic region** rather than any single station. Full syndrome write-up: `opmcq-c14-2`.\n\n| Option | Why it belongs in the key |\n|---|---|\n| **Pancoast tumor** | Apical lung tumour invading the second-order neurone — flagged life-threatening. |\n| **Thoracic aortic aneurysm** | Chest-level compression of the chain — flagged life-threatening. |\n| **Cervical rib** | Thoracic-outlet compression — on the printed list. |\n| **None of the above** | Three of the above are printed causes. |\n\n*(Back-reference repaired: the bank prints "In the previous case…" — the antecedent is Q9 (`opmcq-c14-9`), and the restated stem carries only that case’s findings, not its diagnosis.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Horner syndrome causes span the whole pathway — Pancoast tumour, thoracic aortic aneurysm and cervical rib are all on the book’s list, the first two flagged life-threatening.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.93 (Ch.14 Q10, a back-reference to Q9)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'A **32-year-old woman** presented with **eyelid drooping** and deviation of the eye **outwards and downwards**, on examination there was **unilateral mydriasis**. Paralysis of which of the following nerves is responsible for her condition?',
  options:['Oculomotor nerve','Trochlear nerve','Abducent nerve','Facial nerve'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Ptosis + a down-and-out eye + a dilated pupil is the complete oculomotor (III) palsy, feature for feature.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.182`: the third nerve carries the **levator** (so its palsy gives ptosis — often masking the diplopia), the **sphincter pupillae parasympathetics** (so compressive palsy gives **mydriasis**), and four of the six extraocular muscles — with elevation and adduction lost, the intact **lateral rectus (VI)** and **superior oblique (IV)** pull the eye **out and down**. A *dilated* pupil in a 32-year-old flips the pupil rule of `opmcq-c14-4` to its dangerous column — pupil involved → **trauma, aneurysm, tumour** — making this the presentation in which a posterior communicating artery aneurysm must be actively excluded.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Trochlear nerve** | One muscle (superior oblique): vertical diplopia, hypertropia, head tilt — no ptosis, no pupil sign (`opmcq-c14-7`). |\n| **Abducent nerve** | One muscle (lateral rectus): esotropia and failed abduction — no ptosis, no pupil sign (`opmcq-c14-5`). And here the lateral rectus is demonstrably *working*, since it is dragging the eye outwards. |\n| **Facial nerve** | Closes the lids (orbicularis); its palsy gives **lagophthalmos** — inability to close — the opposite lid problem, and no motility or pupil signs. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Ptosis + eye deviated down and out + mydriasis = third (oculomotor) nerve palsy; the involved pupil marks it compressive until an aneurysm is excluded.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.93 (Ch.14 Q11)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
  stem:'All of the following can cause **chronic retrobulbar neuritis** except?',
  options:['Chloramphenicol','Vitamin B12 deficiency','Giant cell arteritis','Tobacco-alcohol amblyopia','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Chronic retrobulbar neuritis is the book’s name for nutritional/toxic optic neuropathy — and giant cell arteritis is a different disease altogether.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.190` defines it: a **usually bilateral**, slowly progressive optic neuropathy whose printed aetiology is **vitamin B12 deficiency**, **tobacco–alcohol amblyopia**, and **drugs, e.g. ethambutol and chloramphenicol** — three of the four options, lifted verbatim. Its field signature is the **centrocaecal scotoma** (`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 maps "Chronic RB neuritis → centrocecal scotoma"). **Giant cell arteritis** is the exception: an *arteritic ischaemic* optic neuropathy — acute, unilateral, catastrophic, in the elderly (`opmcq-c14-3`) — not a chronic toxic/nutritional process.\n\n| Option | Why it CAN cause it |\n|---|---|\n| **Chloramphenicol** | Named in the book’s drug list (with ethambutol). |\n| **Vitamin B12 deficiency** | Named nutritional cause — the classic centrocaecal scotoma. |\n| **Tobacco-alcohol amblyopia** | Named — the combined toxic/nutritional insult of heavy smoking and drinking. |\n| **None of the above** | Wrong because one option (GCA) is a true exception. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Chronic retrobulbar neuritis = toxic/nutritional optic neuropathy (B12 deficiency, tobacco-alcohol, ethambutol/chloramphenicol) with centrocaecal scotomas; GCA is an acute arteritic ischaemia, not a cause.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.93 (Ch.14 Q12)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'All of the following are **benign forms of nystagmus** except?',
  options:['End-gaze nystagmus','Nystagmus in primary position of gaze','Drug-induced nystagmus','Congenital nystagmus','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The book tabulates exactly three benign forms, and "nystagmus in the primary position" is its stated marker of pathology.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.186`: "**Pathologic forms of nystagmus are usually seen in the primary position of gaze**" — an acquired nystagmus present with the eyes looking straight ahead earns a full neurological evaluation and MRI, and typically announces itself with **oscillopsia** or vertigo. The three benign forms:\n\n| Benign form | Printed features |\n|---|---|\n| **End-gaze** | Fine jerks at the extremes of gaze in normal people — fatigues, absent in primary position |\n| **Drug-induced** | Antiepileptics, barbiturates, sedatives — may be present in all positions of gaze |\n| **Congenital** | Pendular or jerk, onset at birth or in the first 6 months, subnormal vision, and **NO oscillopsia** (the brain has never known a still image) |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **End-gaze / Drug-induced / Congenital** | The book’s three benign forms, verbatim. |\n| **None of the above** | Wrong — option B is genuinely pathological. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'End-gaze, drug-induced and congenital nystagmus are the three benign forms; nystagmus in the primary position of gaze is pathological and buys neuroimaging.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.93–94 (Ch.14 Q13; options D and E printed on p.94)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'Which of the following is best described as **(loss of either superior or inferior half of the visual field in both eyes)**?',
  options:['Scotoma','Hemianopia','Altitudinal hemianopia','Homonymous hemianopia','Bitemporal hemianopia'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**"Altitudinal" is the horizontal-meridian hemianopia — the field lost above or below, not left or right.** This entry carries the full terminology table from `ophthalmology.pdf p.195`; its sibling `opmcq-c14-22` (same five options, different definition in the brackets) points back here.\n\n| Term | Book definition |\n|---|---|\n| **Scotoma** | An area of reduced or lost retinal sensitivity **surrounded by areas of normal sensitivity** |\n| **Hemianopia** | Loss of half the visual field — usually the right or left half in both eyes |\n| **Altitudinal hemianopia** | Loss of the **superior or inferior half** of the visual field |\n| **Homonymous hemianopia** | Loss of either the **right or the left half** of the visual field **in both eyes** |\n| **Bitemporal (heteronymous) hemianopia** | Loss of the **temporal half** of the field in both eyes |\n\nClinical anchors: an **altitudinal** defect is the signature of **non-arteritic AION** (`ophthalmology.pdf p.191`; `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 maps "AION → altitudinal defect") — an optic nerve head event, since the short posterior ciliary circulation supplies the disc in upper and lower halves. **Homonymous** defects localise **behind the chiasm** (most commonly stroke — `opmcq-c14-27`), and **bitemporal** to the **chiasm** (pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma, parasellar meningioma, `ophthalmology.pdf p.195`).\n\nThe watch-point between the two trickiest rows: *altitudinal* splits the field along the **horizontal** meridian (superior/inferior), *homonymous* along the **vertical** (right/left, same side in both eyes) — the stem’s bracket names "superior or inferior", so C.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Altitudinal hemianopia = loss of the superior or inferior half of the field (horizontal meridian) — the NAION signature; right-or-left loss in both eyes is homonymous.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.94 (Ch.14 Q14)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
  stem:'All of the following are characteristic of **optic disc swelling** except?',
  options:['Indistinct disc margins','Vascular tortuosity','Central cup','Capillary hyperemia and hemorrhages','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**A swollen disc LOSES its cup — the presence of a central cup is the odd one out.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.187` characterises optic disc swelling by five features: **1. indistinct disc margins, 2. elevation, 3. vascular tortuosity, 4. ABSENCE of a central cup (obliterated cup), 5. capillary hyperaemia and haemorrhages.** Options A, B and D are three of the five verbatim; option C inverts the fourth — the swelling fills the physiological cup, so an intact central cup argues *against* true disc oedema. (The reverse extreme is glaucomatous atrophy, where the cup *enlarges* — `opmcq-c14-16`.) Whether a swollen disc is papilledema or papillitis is the next discrimination — the full table lives at `opmcq-c14-21`.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Indistinct disc margins** | Feature 1, verbatim. |\n| **Vascular tortuosity** (the bank prints "trotuosity") | Feature 3. |\n| **Capillary hyperemia and hemorrhages** | Feature 5. |\n| **None of the above** | Wrong — C is a genuine exception. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Disc swelling = indistinct margins, elevation, vascular tortuosity, obliterated (absent) cup, and capillary hyperaemia with haemorrhages — a preserved central cup is against it.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.94 (Ch.14 Q15; the bank prints "trotuosity")' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
  stem:'Which of the following is meant by **(secondary optic atrophy)**?',
  options:['Damage to optic nerve secondary to retinal diseases','Damage to optic nerve due to long-standing papilledema','Damage to optic nerve outside of the eyeball','Damage to optic nerve by an end-stage chronic glaucoma','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Every option is one of the four types of optic atrophy — the question is whether you can match each name to its definition.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.194` (with the four fundus captions on `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 14):\n\n| Type | Definition (book) | Fundus (L21 captions) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Primary** | Damage **outside the eyeball** — congenital, MS (recurrent attacks), syphilis, compression by intracranial/orbital tumours; occurs **without previous disc swelling** | Chalky white disc, **sharp** margins |\n| **2. Secondary** | Due to **long-standing papilledema or papillitis** — **preceded by swelling** of the optic nerve head | Dirty grey disc, **blurred** margins |\n| **3. Consecutive** | Due to **retinal diseases** — e.g. retinitis pigmentosa, degenerative myopia | Waxy yellow disc, attenuated vessels |\n| **4. Glaucomatous** | **End-stage chronic glaucoma** — advanced glaucomatous cupping | Pallor confined to an enlarged cup, neuro-retinal rim lost |\n\nSo option B is the definition of *secondary* atrophy; A defines **consecutive**, C defines **primary**, D defines **glaucomatous** — each wrong only because it names a different type. The single discriminating fact between primary and secondary is **whether the disc swelled first**: primary atrophies without ever having swollen, secondary is the scar of swelling. Common to all four (`ophthalmology.pdf p.194`): the disc is **pale from decreased vascularity**, and **there is no treatment**.\n\n*(The bank prints "ouside" for "outside" in option C — corrected here, printed form recorded.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Secondary optic atrophy = atrophy after long-standing papilledema/papillitis (swelling precedes it); primary = damage outside the globe with no prior swelling, consecutive = retinal disease, glaucomatous = end-stage cupping.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.94 (Ch.14 Q16; the bank prints "ouside")' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'Which of the following conditions is characterized by **tertiary syphilis with midbrain affection**?',
  options:['Horner syndrome','Argyll-Robertson syndrome','Oculomotor palsy','Dorsal midbrain syndrome'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The stem is the book’s own highlighted definition, word for word.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.181` opens its Argyll-Robertson section with "**Tertiary syphilis with midbrain affection**" (highlighted), then: **bilateral** (may be asymmetric), **small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation**; investigations are **serologic testing for syphilis plus MRI of the brainstem**. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 8 adds the lesion site and the wider cause list — "Affection of **pretectal area** / Small pupils / Light Near dissociation / Neurosyphilis / **DM, Alcoholism, Encephalitis**". The mnemonic: **ARP — Accommodation Reflex Present, Pupillary (light) Reflex Absent**. The clinical vignette version of this fact is `opmcq-c14-28`.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Horner syndrome** | An oculosympathetic pathway lesion (miosis + ptosis + anhidrosis) — nothing to do with syphilis or the midbrain (`opmcq-c14-2`). |\n| **Oculomotor palsy** | A III nerve lesion — ptosis, mydriasis, down-and-out eye (`opmcq-c14-11`); its pupil is *dilated and fixed to everything*, not light-near dissociated. |\n| **Dorsal midbrain syndrome** | The near-miss: also midbrain, also light-near dissociation — but its causes are **pineal tumours, stroke, MS and hydrocephalus**, not syphilis, and it adds upgaze palsy, convergence-retraction nystagmus and lid retraction (`opmcq-c14-29`). "Tertiary syphilis" is the discriminating token. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Argyll-Robertson pupil = tertiary syphilis with midbrain (pretectal) affection — bilateral small irregular pupils, Accommodation Reflex Present, Pupillary light Reflex Absent.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.94 (Ch.14 Q17)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'Which of the following is a cause of **dilated fixed pupil**?',
  options:['Morphine','Horner syndrome','Stage 3 anaesthesia','Pontine hemorrhage'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The bank keys C — and both course sources put stage III anaesthesia on the MIOSIS side of the ledger. Defective key, recorded as printed.** `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 3 lists under miosis: "Anaesthesia: **3rd stage**"; slide 4 gives mydriasis to "Anaesthesia: **2nd, 4th stages**". `ophthalmology.pdf p.179` agrees in its abnormal-pupil-size table — **Stage II and IV anaesthesia** under *Dilated Fixed Pupil*, **Stage III anaesthesia** under *Small Pupil* — with the book’s own red note: "**II and IV dilate, III constricts**." The key survives only by elimination, because the other three options are all *unambiguous miosis causes*, so C is the single option that could even be argued toward dilation. The `answer` field stays on the bank’s printed key per project rule; learn the sources’ version:\n\n| Cause | Pupil (both sources) |\n|---|---|\n| **Morphine** | **Pin-point** miosis (`L22` sl.3; `ophthalmology.pdf p.179` pin-point list: morphine, organophosphorus, pontine haemorrhage) |\n| **Horner syndrome** | Miosis — sympathetic denervation (`opmcq-c14-2`) |\n| **Pontine hemorrhage** | **Pin-point** pupils |\n| **Anaesthesia stages** | **II and IV dilate, III constricts** — so stage III is a MIOSIS cause |\n\nThe true dilated-fixed-pupil causes the book prints (`ophthalmology.pdf p.179`): traumatic mydriasis, pharmacologic dilatation, ciliary ganglion disease, **oculomotor (III) paralysis**, dorsal midbrain syndrome, **stage II and IV anaesthesia**, and death (bilateral).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bank keys stage III anaesthesia as a dilated-pupil cause, but L22 and the book both teach "II and IV dilate, III constricts" — morphine, Horner and pontine haemorrhage are all miosis causes.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.94–95 (Ch.14 Q18; stem on p.94, options and key on p.95 — the key contradicts L22 sl.3–4 and ophthalmology.pdf p.179, recorded above)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'What is the most common cause of **bilateral abducent nerve palsy**?',
  options:['Intracranial tumor','Elevated intracranial pressure','Myasthenia gravis','Multiple sclerosis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Raised intracranial pressure is the classic producer of BILATERAL sixth nerve palsies — the "false localizing sign".** `ophthalmology.pdf p.184` lists increased ICP (pseudotumor cerebri, hydrocephalus) among sixth nerve causes and states it **can cause bilateral VI palsy**; the visual-pathways part repeats it in a highlighted box (`ophthalmology.pdf p.195`): "**Sixth nerve affection can also occur due to increased intracranial pressure (A false localizing sign)**." The mechanism is mechanical, not local: the abducens has the **longest intracranial course** and climbs over the petrous ridge, so generalised pressure or brainstem shift stretches both nerves at once — the palsy then *falsely* points to a lesion at the nerve when the real problem is diffuse pressure. Pair this with papilledema (`opmcq-c14-20`): a patient with bilateral abduction deficits and swollen discs has raised ICP until proven otherwise.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Intracranial tumor** | Causes raised ICP — but as *one* cause among many; the pressure itself, whatever its source (tumour, hydrocephalus, idiopathic intracranial hypertension), is the common mechanism the question wants. |\n| **Myasthenia gravis** | Can mimic any motility palsy including bilateral abduction weakness (`ophthalmology.pdf p.185`) — but it is a mimic, not a nerve palsy, and not the most common cause. |\n| **Multiple sclerosis** | A demyelinating cause of ocular motor palsies, not the classic bilateral-VI producer. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bilateral abducent palsy = raised intracranial pressure stretching the longest-coursed cranial nerve on both sides — the false localizing sign.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.95 (Ch.14 Q19)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
  stem:'Which of the following can contribute to **papilledema**?',
  options:['Space occupying lesion','Cerebral hemorrhage','Dural sinus thrombosis','Idiopathic','All of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Papilledema is disc swelling secondary to raised intracranial pressure, and every option is on the book’s aetiology list.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.188`: papilledema is **bilateral** disc swelling **secondary to increased ICP**, caused by **brain tumours and space-occupying lesions, cerebral trauma or hemorrhage, meningitis and encephalitis, dural sinus thrombosis, and idiopathic intracranial hypertension** — options A through D verbatim, hence E. The idiopathic entry is pseudotumor cerebri (`ophthalmology.pdf p.189`): typically **women aged 20–40 with recent weight gain**, treated with carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, weight loss, shunts or optic nerve sheath fenestration. Work-up order matters: **imaging (MRI/CT) first, then lumbar puncture** — an LP before excluding a mass risks herniation. Field sign: the **enlarged blind spot** (`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4); vision is otherwise long preserved, with transient obscurations — the discrimination from papillitis is tabulated at `opmcq-c14-21`.\n\n| Option | Why it belongs |\n|---|---|\n| **Space occupying lesion** | The archetypal cause — tumour raising ICP. |\n| **Cerebral hemorrhage** (the bank prints "Cerebra") | Printed on the book’s list with cerebral trauma. |\n| **Dural sinus thrombosis** | Blocks CSF drainage — printed cause. |\n| **Idiopathic** | Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri). |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Papilledema = bilateral disc swelling from raised ICP — SOL, cerebral trauma/hemorrhage, meningoencephalitis, dural sinus thrombosis and idiopathic intracranial hypertension all cause it.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.95 (Ch.14 Q20; the bank prints "Cerebra hemorrhage")' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
  stem:'Which of the following can differentiate **papillitis from papilledema**?',
  options:['Papillitis is usually unilateral','Papillitis is usually associated with decreased vision','Papillitis is usually associated with RAPD','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All three statements are true, and together they are most of the discriminating table.** This is the reference entry for the papillitis-vs-papilledema table — `opmcq-c14-15` and `opmcq-c14-20` point here. From `ophthalmology.pdf p.189` and the handwritten table on `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8:\n\n| | **Papilledema** | **Papillitis** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aetiology | **Raised ICP** | MS, viral, toxic, nutritional |\n| Laterality | **Bilateral** (exception: Foster Kennedy) | **Unilateral** (exception: NMO) |\n| Symptoms | **Transient obscurations** (seconds) | **Marked drop in vision** |\n| VA / colour / contrast | Normal (long preserved) | **Reduced** — defective colour vision |\n| Field | **Enlarged blind spot** | **Central scotoma** |\n| RAPD | **No** | **Yes** — marked |\n| Vitreous | Clear | **Inflammatory cells** |\n| Disc elevation | Marked (+++) | Mild (+) |\n\nThe logic behind the three keyed rows: papilledema is a *pressure* phenomenon transmitted down both optic nerve sheaths — so it is bilateral and the axons work normally for a long time (normal VA, no RAPD). Papillitis is an *inflammation of one nerve* — so it is unilateral, vision falls early and hard, and the asymmetric conduction produces a relative afferent pupillary defect (the Marcus Gunn pupil of `opmcq-c14-25`).\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Unilateral** | True — table row 2. |\n| **Decreased vision** | True — row 3–4. |\n| **RAPD** | True — row 6. |\n| **None of the above** | Three of the above are printed discriminators. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Papillitis is unilateral with early visual loss, central scotoma and RAPD; papilledema is bilateral with preserved vision, enlarged blind spots and no RAPD.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.95 (Ch.14 Q21)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'Which of the following is best described as **(loss of either right or left half of the visual field in both eyes)**?',
  options:['Scotoma','Hemianopia','Altitudinal hemianopia','Homonymous hemianopia','Bitemporal hemianopia'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**"Right or left half in both eyes" is the book’s definition of HOMONYMOUS hemianopia, verbatim.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.195`: "Loss of either the right or the left half of the visual field in both eyes." The full five-term table lives at `opmcq-c14-14` — this is its paired sibling: the bank prints the **same five options** for both questions and moves only the bracketed definition, so the discriminating tokens are **"right or left"** here versus **"superior or inferior"** there. Vertical meridian → homonymous; horizontal meridian → altitudinal.\n\nLocalisation is the point of the term: a homonymous defect means the lesion is **behind the chiasm** — optic tract, lateral geniculate body, optic radiation or occipital cortex (`ophthalmology.pdf p.195`), where each hemisphere carries the *same-sided* half of both retinas’ world. Most common cause: **stroke** (`opmcq-c14-27`).\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Scotoma** | A defect *surrounded by normal field*, not half the field. |\n| **Hemianopia** | The generic parent term — the bracket demands the specific one ("in both eyes", right/left half). The bank uses the bare word as the umbrella and keys the precise term. |\n| **Altitudinal hemianopia** | Superior or inferior half — Q14’s answer, the wrong meridian here. |\n| **Bitemporal hemianopia** | The **temporal** half in each eye — heteronymous, a chiasmal pattern, not "right or left". |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Homonymous hemianopia = loss of the same (right or left) half of the field in both eyes — a retrochiasmal lesion, most commonly stroke.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.95 (Ch.14 Q22; same option menu as Q14 with a different bracketed definition — paired questions, not a duplicate)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'A **27-year-old female** presented with **lid drooping and double vision**, she reports **generalized weakness and easy fatigue**, on examination there were **intact pupils** with marked **bilateral ptosis** and affection of ocular motility. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:['Oculomotor nerve palsy','Trochlear nerve palsy','Myasthenia gravis','Dorsal midbrain syndrome'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Bilateral fatigable ptosis + variable motility deficit + INTACT PUPILS in a young woman is myasthenia gravis.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.185`: MG is an **autoimmune disorder of neuromuscular transmission** (antibodies against the acetylcholine receptor); **ptosis and diplopia are the presenting complaint in about half** of patients; it can **simulate any ocular motor palsy**; weakness **worsens with sustained effort** and through the day — the "easy fatigue" and generalized weakness in this stem. And the book prints the deciding sign in capitals: "**THE PUPIL IS NEVER AFFECTED IN MG**" — the sphincter and dilator are smooth muscle outside the nicotinic junction. The Tutorial box adds the work-up (ice test — ptosis improves after 2 minutes of cooling — edrophonium/Tensilon test, AChR antibodies, EMG) and treatment (anticholinesterases, steroids, immunosuppressives).\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Oculomotor nerve palsy** | Ptosis, yes — but typically **unilateral**, with the eye down-and-out, and in the dangerous compressive form a **dilated** pupil (`opmcq-c14-11`). Bilateral ptosis with normal pupils and fatigue is the junction, not the nerve. |\n| **Trochlear nerve palsy** | No ptosis at all — vertical diplopia and head tilt (`opmcq-c14-7`). |\n| **Dorsal midbrain syndrome** | Lid **retraction** (Collier sign), not ptosis, plus upgaze palsy and light-near-dissociated pupils (`opmcq-c14-29`) — the lids and pupils both point away. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Fatigable bilateral ptosis + variable ophthalmoplegia + generalized weakness with NEVER-affected pupils = myasthenia gravis, the great motility mimic.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.96 (Ch.14 Q23)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'A **39-year-old male** presented with **unilateral miosis**, **lid drooping** and **apparent enophthalmos**, which of the following is the most appropriate investigation to confirm the diagnosis?',
  options:['MRI abdomen','MRI thorax','Echocardiography','CT brain'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Horner syndrome again (the vignette of `opmcq-c14-9`, restated in full by the bank) — and the confirming investigation goes where the dangerous causes live: the chest.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.180` prescribes the Horner work-up as "**MRI from the base of the brain to the mid-thoracic region**" plus carotid angiography — imaging that follows the whole sympathetic pathway of `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 10 (hypothalamus → T1 → over the lung apex → up the neck). Of the four options, **MRI thorax** is the one that covers the flagged life-threatening territory: the **Pancoast tumour at the lung apex** and the **thoracic aortic aneurysm** (`opmcq-c14-10`), and it also catches a cervical rib’s level at the thoracic outlet. Many cases remain idiopathic after imaging — but that verdict is only allowed once the chest is clean.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **MRI abdomen** | The oculosympathetic pathway never enters the abdomen — nothing there produces Horner. |\n| **Echocardiography** | Images the heart, not the sympathetic chain; even the aortic aneurysm of concern is best shown by MRI/CT of the thorax, not echo. |\n| **CT brain** | The first-order neurone does start centrally, but an isolated brain scan misses the apex of the lung and the neck — the sites where the treatable, killing causes sit. The book’s own protocol spans brain base **to mid-thorax**, and the single best pick from this menu is the thorax. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Confirming the cause of a Horner syndrome means imaging the sympathetic pathway’s dangerous stations — MRI of the thorax for the lung apex (Pancoast) and thoracic aorta.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.96 (Ch.14 Q24; the bank restates the Q9 vignette in full — not a back-reference)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'**Marcus Gunn pupil** is seen in which of the following conditions?',
  options:['Cataract','Compressive optic neuropathy','Scleritis','AMD'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**A Marcus Gunn pupil is a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD), and an RAPD is the fingerprint of OPTIC NERVE disease — of this list, compressive optic neuropathy.** `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 6 shows the swinging-flashlight test: light swung from the healthy to the affected eye produces paradoxical **dilatation**, because the diseased nerve delivers a weaker afferent signal than the one just removed. `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 3 lists RAPD among the cardinal "manifestations of optic nerve dysfunction" (with reduced acuity, colour and contrast and a field defect), and slide 4 maps **compression → sector/wedge field defect**. The rule that decides every option here: **an RAPD requires asymmetric AFFERENT (optic nerve, or overwhelming retinal) disease — media opacities and ocular surface disease never produce one.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Cataract** | A media opacity — even a dense cataract does not reduce the afferent pupillomotor signal enough to give an RAPD; light scatters through. A cataract with an RAPD means something else is wrong behind it. |\n| **Scleritis** | Painful inflammation of the ocular wall; the optic nerve conducts normally — no RAPD. |\n| **AMD** | A macular disease; an RAPD needs *extensive* retinal damage, and typical AMD — even with a poor acuity — spares enough retina that no clinically useful RAPD appears. *(That grading point is not taken from the course material; the course sources list RAPD under optic nerve dysfunction and print no retinal exception.)* |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Marcus Gunn pupil = RAPD on the swinging-flashlight test = asymmetric optic nerve disease (e.g. compressive optic neuropathy); cataract and macular disease do not produce it.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.96 (Ch.14 Q25; the bank prints "Marcus gunn")' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'Which of the following is a cause of **pinpoint pupil**?',
  options:['Optic neuritis','Third nerve palsy','Organophosphorus poisoning','Acute angle-closure glaucoma'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The book’s pin-point list has three members — morphine, organophosphorus, pontine haemorrhage — and only one of them is on this menu.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.179` files "pin-point" as the extreme of the small-pupil column: **Morphine, Organophosphorus, Pontine haemorrhage**. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 3 teaches the same class as "**Pesticides**" among the parasympathomimetic miosis causes — organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine floods the sphincter pupillae and clamps the pupil down (with the rest of the cholinergic toxidrome: salivation, lacrimation, sweating, bradycardia).\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Optic neuritis** | An afferent lesion — it gives an **RAPD** (`opmcq-c14-25`), not a small pupil; if anything the affected pupil reacts *less*. `L22` slide 4 actually lists optic neuritis among **mydriasis** causes. |\n| **Third nerve palsy** | The efferent parasympathetics are cut — **dilated** fixed pupil, the exact opposite (`opmcq-c14-11`; `L22` sl.4 "3rd nerve paralysis"). |\n| **Acute angle-closure glaucoma** | `L22` slide 4 lists acute congestive glaucoma under **mydriasis** — the classic mid-dilated, vertically oval, fixed pupil of the pressure-ischaemic sphincter. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Pinpoint pupils = morphine, organophosphorus poisoning or pontine haemorrhage; third nerve palsy and acute angle-closure give DILATED pupils, optic neuritis gives an RAPD.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.96 (Ch.14 Q26)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'What is the most common cause of **homonymous hemianopia**?',
  options:['Trauma','Mass lesions','Stroke','Glaucoma'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The book ranks the causes in one sentence: "Stroke is the most common cause of homonymous hemianopia, followed by trauma and mass lesions."** (`ophthalmology.pdf p.195`.) A homonymous defect localises **behind the chiasm** — optic tract, lateral geniculate body, optic radiation or occipital cortex (`opmcq-c14-22`) — and that territory is exactly what the middle and posterior cerebral arteries perfuse, so vascular occlusion dominates the aetiology. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 24 adds the localising refinements: a **tract** lesion gives an *incongruous* contralateral homonymous hemianopia, while **occipital cortex** lesions give a *congruous* one **with macular sparing**. The book also notes the company such lesions keep: papilledema from mass effect, and III/IV/VI palsies if the nerves or nuclei are caught.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Trauma** | Second on the book’s printed ranking. |\n| **Mass lesions** | Third on the ranking. |\n| **Glaucoma** | Not a pathway lesion at all — glaucomatous field loss is *nerve-fibre-bundle* loss (arcuate scotomas, nasal steps) respecting the **horizontal** meridian, never a homonymous pattern respecting the vertical one. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Stroke is the most common cause of homonymous hemianopia, ahead of trauma and mass lesions — the retrochiasmal pathway is vascular territory.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.96 (Ch.14 Q27)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
  stem:'A **45-year-old man** is found to have **small irregular pupils** that **do not constrict to light but constrict normally during accommodation**. Extraocular movements and visual acuity are normal. He has a history of **untreated syphilis**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Adie’s tonic pupil','Argyll Robertson pupil','Horner syndrome','Pharmacologic miosis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Small, irregular, light-near dissociated pupils on a background of untreated syphilis — the stem walks through the Argyll-Robertson definition point by point.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.181`: **tertiary syphilis with midbrain affection**; **bilateral** (may be asymmetric) **small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation**; confirmed by **serologic testing plus MRI of the brainstem**. The mnemonic is in the name: **ARP — Accommodation Reflex Present, Pupillary (light) Reflex Absent** — exactly this patient’s "constrict during accommodation, not to light". The lesion sits in the **pretectal area** (`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 8), where the light-reflex fibres are interrupted while the more ventral near-response pathway is spared — which is the anatomy behind every light-near dissociation. The recall version of this fact is `opmcq-c14-17`.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Adie’s tonic pupil** | The other classic light-near dissociation — but a **large**, regular pupil (usually unilateral, young women, absent deep tendon reflexes) that redilates slowly and constricts *tonically* to near; nothing about it is small or irregular. *(Adie’s is not taken from the course material — neither the book chapter nor L22 mentions it; supplied to explain the distractor.)* |\n| **Horner syndrome** | Small pupil, yes — but **round and regular, unilateral, reacting normally to light**, with ptosis and anhidrosis alongside (`opmcq-c14-2`); no light-near dissociation. |\n| **Pharmacologic miosis** | Pilocarpine-type miosis is symmetric, **round**, and the pupil fails to move for *near as well as light* — dissociation is exactly what drugs do not produce. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bilateral small IRREGULAR pupils with light-near dissociation + syphilis history = Argyll Robertson pupils (ARP: Accommodation Reflex Present, Pupillary light Reflex Absent).',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.97 (Ch.14 Q28)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c14-29', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
  stem:'A **28-year-old woman** presents with **difficulty looking up**. Examination shows **impaired upward gaze**, **light-near dissociation** of the pupils, and **convergence-retraction nystagmus** on attempted upgaze. She also has **lid retraction (Collier sign)**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Internuclear ophthalmoplegia','Dorsal midbrain syndrome','Myasthenia gravis','Third nerve palsy'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Upgaze palsy + light-near dissociation + convergence-retraction nystagmus + Collier sign is the dorsal midbrain (Parinaud) syndrome — the stem prints the book’s "3 Ls" in full.** `ophthalmology.pdf p.180`: the **3 Ls** are **L**ight-near dissociation, **L**oss of upgaze, and **L**id retraction (**Collier sign**), with convergence-retraction nystagmus on attempted upgaze; causes are **hydrocephalus, compressive lesions (pineal tumours), MS, stroke and midbrain haemorrhage**, and **MRI is mandatory** — in a 28-year-old woman, a pineal region tumour and MS lead the list. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 9 teaches the same set: "Pineal body tumors, Stroke, MS / Light Near dissociation / Upward gaze paralysis / Convergence-retraction nystagmus / Lid retraction". Note the deck’s pairing: its only two light-near dissociations are Argyll-Robertson (`opmcq-c14-28`) and this — pupils small and irregular with syphilis, versus upgaze palsy with lid retraction.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Internuclear ophthalmoplegia** | An MLF lesion — impaired **adduction** with abducting nystagmus of the other eye on *horizontal* gaze; no upgaze palsy, no pupil sign, no lid retraction. *(INO is not taken from the course material — this chapter never describes it; supplied to explain the distractor.)* |\n| **Myasthenia gravis** | Fatigable **ptosis** — the lids droop, they do not retract — and "the pupil is never affected in MG" (`ophthalmology.pdf p.185`; `opmcq-c14-23`), while this patient’s pupils are dissociated. |\n| **Third nerve palsy** | Loses elevation, but with ptosis, mydriasis and a down-and-out eye (`opmcq-c14-11`) — not bilateral upgaze palsy with retracted lids and convergence-retraction nystagmus. |\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-neuro — the light-near dissociation of the pupils is half of this syndrome’s signature.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Dorsal midbrain (Parinaud) syndrome = the 3 Ls — Light-near dissociation, Loss of upgaze, Lid retraction (Collier sign) — plus convergence-retraction nystagmus; MRI is mandatory (pineal tumours, hydrocephalus, MS, stroke).',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.97 (Ch.14 Q29, the last question of the chapter)' }
